
## Traces and Sets of Traces

### Traces

### Sets of Traces

{% if cg.has_generator_functions() -%}
The traces are stored by the class `TraceSetBase`.
This class has two child classes that are actually instantiated in the code: `TraceSet` and `SharedTraceSet`.
{%- else -%}
The traces are stored by the class `TraceSet`, which is a sub-class of `TraceSetBase` that
actually keeps the traces in a container.
{% endif %}
The class `TraceSet` represents a set of traces that are being created and used concurrently.
That is, it supports concurrent creation of new traces (the traces are never removed,
so this functionality is not implemented now) with their getters.
It is assumed that there is only one producer (new traces cannot be created concurrently).
In the current code base, this producer is the `CSVReader` that reads files and stores them
into the traces in the `TraceSet`.

{% if cg.has_generator_functions() -%}
The class `SharedTraceSet` is used by the generator functions. Instances of this class are created
by `Function` classes and hold the traces. The traces cannot be accessed through this class, though,
but must be accessed through the class `TraceSetView`. Monitors create `TraceSetView`s referencing
a `SharedTraceSet` instance and the `TraceSetView` then allows to iterate over the traces stored
in the `SharedTraceSet` instance. `TraceSetView` remembers what traces were already used by a monitor
and so in this way, multiple monitors can access traces in the same `SharedTraceSet`. Also, reading
traces through `TraceSetView`s never runs concurrently with creating the traces in `Function`,
so `SharedTraceSet` is not concurrent and cannot be used concurrently unless the user takes care
of sequentializing the accesses using, e.g., locks.
{%- endif %}
